Z||000002||Monkeyman||11-29-2001||05:07 AM||||I ended up using GoBack to reset it to the way it was yesterday morning.  I only wanted to go back a few hours but when I clicked on that, all I got was alternating white and black screens.  Odd thing (may or may not be related).  When I did a defrag last night (after all of this mess), it would constantly get to around 85% then say that the disc had changed and start over only to jump back up to 85% again.  Maybe it's time to save everything to CD and wipe the HD clean.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cliff Pennock||11-29-2001||06:13 AM||||Did you password protect your computer or did you password protect Windows? The first is done in the computer's BIOS, the second in Windows. If you password protected Windows, then a new user has been created and all your passwords from the previous (empty) user will not be available - which does make sense.<P>Running ScanDisk in Windows will only work if no other programs are running because as soon as Windows "thinks" the contents on your HD has been changed, it will start over again. Best way to do a Scandisk is to restart your computer in Dos Mode (Win9x) and type "scandisk" at the dos prompt.||||reg||1
